From: Project Bluefin factory <noreply@projectbluefin.io>
Subject: [PATCH] tests: let meson bound pw-test-endpoint, not a 5-second alarm

test-endpoint.c arms its own watchdog before doing the same context setup
and connect that its sibling tests do:

    alarm(5); /* watchdog; terminate after 5 seconds */

On this factory's build root that setup costs more than five seconds, so
the test kills itself part way through and meson reports

    39/52 pipewire:pw-test-endpoint  FAIL  5.01s  killed by signal 14 SIGALRM

with all 51 other tests passing. The budget is out of line with its own
siblings rather than with anything this test does: pw-test-stream and
pw-test-filter are declared in the same test_apps list in
src/tests/meson.build, with the same environment, and take 12.02s and
18.03s on the same machine in the same run.

Neither the test nor any assertion is skipped here, and the run is still
bounded: src/tests/meson.build sets no timeout: kwarg on these tests, so
meson's default 30-second per-test timeout applies and kills a genuine
hang well before this alarm. Raising the alarm above that default makes
meson the effective guard instead of a second, tighter one that fires
first on a slow machine.

--- a/src/tests/test-endpoint.c
+++ b/src/tests/test-endpoint.c
@@ -438,7 +438,7 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
 {
 	pw_init(&argc, &argv);
 
-	alarm(5); /* watchdog; terminate after 5 seconds */
+	alarm(60); /* watchdog; meson's 30s per-test timeout fires first */
 	test_endpoint();
 
 	return 0;
